INT. ICU CUBICLE - NIGHT

A ventilator sighs. A woman lies motionless, wires everywhere.

DR. AUGUSTINE BAPTISTE, 50s, reads a monitor. He frowns. NURSE JOSEFINA RIOS, 30s, checks a line and glances at the door. MRS. DELPHINE ACHTERBERG, 70s, the patient's mother, sits in the corner chair gripping her purse.

BAPTISTE
(to the room)
Pressure's dropping.

JOSEFINA
Since eleven.

She adjusts the drip. He leans in. She steps back. He taps the glass. She holds her breath.

BAPTISTE
(muttering)
Come on, come on.

DELPHINE
Is she in pain?

BAPTISTE
No, ma'am.

DELPHINE
Then why does she look like that?

JOSEFINA
(gently)
It's the machine.

BAPTISTE
Josefina, call the family.

JOSEFINA
Which family?

BAPTISTE
All of it.

Delphine rises. Her purse slides to the floor. She stands by the bed and covers her daughter's hand with her own, and the monitor, very softly, begins to slow.
